==============================
LinkedIn Promotion
==============================

Why do so many smart, capable professionals struggle with the "Growth Mindset"?

We understand the concept intellectually. But when faced with ambiguity or failure, our default response is often: "I'm just not the kind of person who is good at this".

I realized recently that this doesn't come from a lack of potential. It comes from school.

If you grew up in an exam-oriented education system, you were trained in a "fixed mindset factory":
❌ Every question has one right answer.
❌ Mistakes aren't learning opportunities; they are deductions.
❌ You are your score.

When you combine that with a culture that views public failure as a loss of "face," adopting a growth mindset doesn't just feel hard. It feels dangerous.

I wrote a piece on how to unlearn this binary trap and separate your identity from your outcomes.

Read it here: [Link]

🔗 https://roobystudio.com/posts/xxx

